The '''Confederal Council of Arbitration''' ({{lang-bl|Consejo Confederal de Arbitrage }}) is a [[confederal institution]], specifically an [[W:Arbitral tribunal|arbitral tribunal]], responsible for facilitating final mediation and arbitration. It differs from a typical [[W:Supreme court|supreme court]] in that it does not have broad authority to impose laws or regulations on individual communities, instead being strictly limited to arbitrating conflicts and disputes that were unable to be [[W:Subsidiarity|resolved at lower levels]] via [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation councils]]. Since its founding in 1921, the Council's jurisdiction has expanded to the entire confederation through the [[W:voluntary association|voluntary consent]] of cantons to participate in and be bound by the decisions of the Council. It has since emerged as an important platform for the facilitating of inter-communal cooperation, as opposed to a mechanism for imposing centralized authority.

The council's composition and procedures are democratically accountable to the member communities. [[W:Arbitrators|Arbitrators]] are directly elected by their communities, and the Council displays items on the day planner of the arbitrators, its daily actions and its sessions on television, [[W:YouTube|YouTube]] and the [[Open Cabildo]].

